\section{Analysis}\label{sec:analysis}

The five-way comparison in \S\ref{sec:experiments} established that gating produces no measurable benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning. This section traces the failure through a three-step causal chain: we first show that gates learn behavior opposite to the design intent (\S\ref{subsec:gate-conditioning}), then rule out gradient conflict as the explanation through a causal exclusion experiment (\S\ref{subsec:pcgrad}), and finally identify distribution shift as the root cause (\S\ref{subsec:dist-shift}). \S\ref{subsec:mechanism-specificity} confirms this selectivity with an orthogonal hidden-state metric.

\subsection{Gate Turn Conditioning}\label{subsec:gate-conditioning}

The design intent of ThreadGate is to modulate the residual stream more aggressively at late conversation turns, where GAR naturally decays (\S\ref{sec:background}). We test whether the learned gates achieve this by comparing gate values at turn~1 (early) and turn~21 (late) across all 28 layers of the trained model.

The gates do the opposite. In 27 of 28 layers, the gate value at turn~1 is lower than at turn~21 (Wilcoxon signed-rank test: $p = 3.73 \times 10^{-8}$, effect size $r = 0.975$). At layer~23, the gate is 0.769 at turn~1 and 0.979 at turn~21; at layer~14, it is 0.747 at turn~1 and 0.928 at turn~21. The pattern is consistent: gates suppress the residual stream more at early turns (where GAR is already high) and become nearly passive at late turns (where intervention is most needed). This inversion has a straightforward explanation. NTP loss pressure is highest at late turns, where the model must predict tokens conditioned on a long context. The NTP gradient pushes gates toward 1.0 to preserve the full sublayer computation, and this pressure is strongest precisely where GAR-based attenuation would be most useful.

The resulting inference-time gate values range from 0.93 to 0.98 (2--7\% attenuation), far below what meaningful modulation would require; this explains the convergence with LoRA-only in Table~\ref{tab:four-way}. Figure~\ref{fig:gate-shift}(a) visualizes the per-layer gate difference. The inverted turn conditioning raises a natural question: does the NTP-GAR gradient conflict prevent gates from learning the intended behavior? If gradient conflict is the root cause, resolving it should rescue gate conditioning.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_gate_shift.pdf}
  \caption{(a)~Gate turn conditioning: per-layer gate values at turn~1 vs turn~21. In 27/28 layers, gates approach 1.0 at late turns, inverting the design intent ($p = 3.73 \times 10^{-8}$). (b)~Distribution shift: the 20--33$\times$ GAR gap persists across all five variants (TG, +PCGrad, UG, LoRA-only, Base), the failure is universal across all configurations.}
  \label{fig:gate-shift}
\end{figure}

\subsection{PCGrad Causal Exclusion}\label{subsec:pcgrad}

The NTP and GAR gradients point in opposing directions across 68--79\% of layers throughout training, with mean cosine similarity ranging from $-$0.089 (epoch~0) to $-$0.042 (epoch~2). The NTP gradient magnitude dominates by 7.5--14.7$\times$. This raises a natural hypothesis: perhaps opposing gradient directions prevent the GAR objective from converging. If so, eliminating direction conflict should prevent the collapse. We test this using PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad}, which projects the gradient of one task onto the normal plane of the other whenever two task gradients conflict. We apply PCGrad to gate parameters only, scoping our claim to direction conflict; magnitude imbalance \citep{chen2018gradnorm} and Nash equilibrium violations \citep{navon2022nash} remain untested.

Eliminating direction conflict via PCGrad shifts per-step cosine similarity from consistently negative ($-$0.089) to oscillating around zero ($-$0.15 to $+$0.09), yet all outcomes remain unchanged: IID GAR-ours (0.50 vs 0.50), PPL (2.780 vs 2.784), and autoregressive GAR (0.015 vs 0.016) are indistinguishable (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}). $\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ follows a nearly identical training trajectory and collapses in the same manner (Figure~\ref{fig:training-pcgrad}b). The causal exclusion follows by \emph{modus tollens}: if direction conflict causes the failure, removing it should prevent it; PCGrad removes it; the failure persists; therefore direction conflict is not the cause. Direction conflict is neither necessary (removing it does not rescue GAR) nor sufficient ($\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ converges during training despite persistent conflict). The 7.5--14.7$\times$ NTP magnitude dominance renders directional interference inconsequential. With gradient conflict ruled out, the question narrows: the training dynamics are not the problem (GAR converges under teacher forcing), so the failure must occur at the transition from training to inference.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_training_pcgrad.pdf}
  \caption{(a)~Gradient cosine similarity between NTP and GAR objectives. v7 (ThreadGate) remains consistently negative; v9 (+PCGrad) oscillates around zero. (b)~IID evaluation outcomes are indistinguishable: eliminating direction conflict changes nothing.}
  \label{fig:training-pcgrad}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Distribution Shift Analysis}\label{subsec:dist-shift}

The preceding analysis establishes two findings: gates are ineffective because NTP pressure pushes them toward identity (\S\ref{subsec:gate-conditioning}), and this ineffectiveness is not caused by gradient direction conflict (\S\ref{subsec:pcgrad}). A separate question remains: why do the GAR improvements achieved during teacher-forced training fail to transfer to autoregressive generation?

Recall from \S\ref{sec:experiments} that teacher-forced GAR-ours reaches 0.50 for all fine-tuned variants, while autoregressive GAR-ours drops to 0.015--0.025, a 20--33$\times$ gap that persists across all training strategies. Under same-distribution evaluation (teacher-forced UltraChat text), GAR-ours is approximately 0.50; the model has learned the intended attention pattern on the training distribution. Under cross-distribution evaluation (model-generated text), GAR-ours collapses universally. The training itself is sound; the failure is one of generalization.

GAR-Dongre provides a revealing control. Across both evaluation conditions, GAR-Dongre remains uniformly low (0.01--0.02) with no distribution gap. The distribution shift selectively destroys the concentrated attention pattern captured by GAR-ours (last-token, summary-position attention) while leaving the diffuse attention pattern captured by GAR-Dongre (averaged across all positions) unaffected. This selectivity implies that the distribution shift disrupts a specific learned routing behavior rather than attention computation in general.

The mechanism connecting training regime to distribution shift is exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled}. During training, the model conditions each token prediction on ground-truth prefixes from UltraChat. At inference, it conditions on its own prior outputs. The attention patterns optimized for the former distribution do not transfer to the latter, because the statistical properties of model-generated text diverge from those of human-written reference text. The 2--7\% gate attenuation documented in \S\ref{subsec:gate-conditioning} is far too weak to compensate for this distributional mismatch.

\citet{he2019exposure} showed that language models exhibit token-level self-recovery from exposure bias: accumulated errors in autoregressive generation do not degrade perplexity as severely as worst-case analysis predicts. Our results are consistent with this finding at the token level, as PPL remains normal across all fine-tuned conditions (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}). The critical distinction is between primary and auxiliary objectives. The NTP objective is robust to exposure bias because next-token prediction is the same task at both training and inference time. The GAR auxiliary objective is fragile because it optimizes an attention pattern under teacher-forced context that has no counterpart in autoregressive generation. Primary-task self-recovery does not extend to auxiliary objectives that depend on distributional properties of the input prefix. Figure~\ref{fig:attention-heatmap} confirms that teacher-forced GAR-ours remains near $\tau = 0.5$ across turns (7--8\% decay over 5 turns, $N$\,=\,45), yet autoregressive GAR collapses to 0.015--0.025 for all variants. Figure~\ref{fig:gate-shift}(b) summarizes the five-model GAR comparison across evaluation conditions.

\subsection{Mechanism Specificity}\label{subsec:mechanism-specificity}

\S\ref{subsec:dist-shift} showed that exposure bias selectively destroys GAR-ours (20--33$\times$ gap) while GAR-Dongre remains unaffected. We test whether this selectivity extends beyond attention with an orthogonal hidden-state probe.

We define Turn-level Residual Coherence (TRC) as the cosine similarity between hidden states at the last-token position of adjacent assistant turns. If exposure bias caused broad representational failure, TRC should show a comparable gap. It does not. For v7 ThreadGate, teacher-forced TRC is 0.334 and autoregressive TRC is 0.332 (gap $= +0.002$). For LoRA-only, teacher-forced TRC is 0.349 and autoregressive TRC is 0.411 (gap $= -0.062$, reversed): autoregressive generation does not degrade hidden-state coherence. Neither checkpoint shows a TRC gap comparable to the 20--33$\times$ GAR collapse.

The slightly elevated autoregressive TRC for LoRA-only likely reflects higher token-level predictability in model-generated text, which produces more self-similar hidden states. These results confirm the selectivity from \S\ref{subsec:dist-shift} with an orthogonal metric: exposure bias disrupts a specific learned routing behavior (summary-position attention to goal tokens) while leaving internal representations intact.

